  "input": "あなたは英語学習コンテンツの独立評価者です。以下の英語記事から「英語上級者が学ぶ価値のあるKey Phrase」を選ぶ課題があり、\n複数の匿名の選定結果(セット S1〜Sn、各5件以内)があります。各候補を下記rubricで採点してください。どのセットがどのモデルの結果かは不明です。\n\n【選定時に参加者へ与えられた条件(参考)】\n1. 本文中の連続した語句だけを抜き出す(一般化以外の変更・言い換え・追加・創作は禁止)。\n2. 一般化後5語以内。\n3. 固有名詞や特定の人物・物事を指す部分は、意味と文法を保って ~ / A / B / someone / something 等に置き換える(一般化できる場合は必ず行う)。\n4. 上級者でも学ぶ価値のある語彙・イディオム・慣用表現を優先する。\n5. 基本語だけで構成され字面どおりに意味が分かる表現や、上級者なら知っている易しい表現は除外する。\n6. 条件を満たす候補から5件選ぶ。適切な候補が不足する場合のみ5件未満とし、数合わせはしない。\n\n【採点rubric(各候補、各0〜2の整数)】\n- G 一般化の適切さ: 2=本文固有の固有名詞・特定の人物/物事が適切に一般化されている、または一般化すべき要素がなく原文のままで適切 / 1=一般化が不十分または過剰、あるいは軽微な不自然さ / 0=一般化すべき固有要素が残って学習価値を損なう、または一般化で意味・文法が壊れている。\n- V 上級者向け学習価値: 2=上級者でも学ぶ価値が明確(句動詞・イディオム・コロケーション・語法・語彙として) / 1=価値はあるが中程度、または一部平易 / 0=学習価値がほぼない。\n- E 易しさの排除: 2=易しい表現ではない(意味が字面から自明でない) / 1=やや易しいが慣用的な意味や語法の学びはある / 0=基本語のみで字面どおり、上級者なら当然知っている。\n- N 単位の自然さ: 2=自然で完結した学習単位 / 1=余分な語または不足があるが許容 / 0=中途半端に切られている・不自然。\n- needs_generalization: その候補の元の表現に、一般化(~, A/B, someone, something等)すべき要素が本文上あったか(true/false)。\nセット単位: padding = 5件を埋めるために学習価値の低い候補(V<=0またはE<=0)を入れていると判断するか(true/false)。\n\n【注意】\n- 本文との一致・語数は別途機械検証するため、ここでは採点対象外。ただし本文に明らかに存在しない表現と気付いた場合のみ noteに \"not_in_text\" と書く。\n- 候補の優劣を、セットの並び順やセットIDから推測しない。各候補を独立に採点する。\n- 候補が0件のセットは candidates を空配列にする。\n\n【出力】JSONのみ(説明文・コードフェンス不要)。noteは通常は空文字、本文に存在しない表現と気付いた場合のみ \"not_in_text\"。形式:\n{\"sets\":[{\"set_id\":\"S1\",\"padding\":false,\"candidates\":[{\"idx\":1,\"G\":2,\"V\":2,\"E\":1,\"N\":2,\"needs_generalization\":false,\"note\":\"\"}]}]}\n\n\n【記事】\n\n# Coffee Bean Prices Fall. Are Price Tags Still on a Journey?\n\n“Beans cost less now!” When you hear that, your wallet pumps its fist. You already imagine your usual cup costing less, too. But a shop’s price may not change right away. When a price tag makes you ask, “Haven’t you heard the news?”, it feels like there is a delay.\n\nThe International Coffee Organization shared a measure of coffee prices. It fell 8.7% from the month before. The group said more beans picked in Brazil had reached the market. It also expected a good supply to continue.\n\nThe US Department of Agriculture said several things had raised prices at once. There was bad weather in Brazil and Vietnam. Fewer beans were for sale. Demand was also strong.\n\nAt the same time, US data on what people pay showed coffee cost 6.1% more than in the same month a year earlier. You might wonder, “Did prices fall or rise?” But this data was for a different month than the bean price measure. It also does not show the price of coffee at a cafe. Don’t put the two numbers in the ring just yet.\n\nSo, how long does it take for bean prices to reach a price tag?\n\nPeople in the market and experts spoke with Reuters. They estimate raw-bean prices take at least about nine months to appear in US shop prices. The road to a price tag is quite a long journey.\n\nThe article says US companies that roast coffee keep raw beans in stock. Roasting and packing the beans also take time. These companies often talk with stores about prices on a regular basis. A shop’s price tag does not change the moment the market moves.\n\nHowever, “about nine months” is an estimate based on interviews. It is not a rule for every company. And it is not a promise that “prices will fall in nine months.” It is too soon to mark the date on your calendar.\n\nA cafe cup also costs money for more than just beans. A person who runs a cafe in Chicago said three costs had pushed prices up. These were cups, workers’ pay, and buying beans. Even if bean prices fall, other costs do not always fall with them.\n\nIf you are waiting for your usual cup to get cheaper, ask more than, “How much do beans cost now?” Also ask, “When does the bean price reach here?” A coffee price tag may have its own hidden clock, separate from the latest market news.\n\n## In one line\nFalling coffee bean prices may take at least nine months to affect US shop prices.\n\n\n【匿名の選定結果】\n\nS1:\n  1. pump one's fist\n  2. raw bean\n  3. right away\n  4. based on interviews\n  5. take time\n\nS2:\n  1. pumps one's fist\n  2. in the ring\n\nS3:\n  1. your wallet pumps its fist\n  2. keep raw beans in stock\n  3. road to a price tag\n  4. have its own hidden clock\n  5. on a regular basis\n\nS4:\n  1. pumps its fist\n  2. keep something in stock\n  3. on a regular basis\n  4. the moment ~ moves\n  5. just yet\n\nS5:\n  1. pumps its fist\n  2. in the ring\n  3. on a regular basis\n  4. a hidden clock\n  5. in one line\n\nS6:\n  1. pumps its fist\n  2. keep ~ in stock\n  3. on a regular basis\n  4. the moment the market moves\n  5. pushed prices up\n\nS7:\n  1. pumps its fist\n  2. on a journey\n  3. put something in the ring\n  4. on a regular basis\n  5. have its own hidden clock\n",
